package seeder

// Register installs __name__'s seeders on r. It is the stable, user-editable
// seam: `weld add seed` writes this file once and never regenerates it, so the
// seeders you add here survive every later `weld add`.
//
// Keep this file a short index. Each seeder lives in its own seed_<name>.go file
// in this package and is registered here, so the run order is readable in one
// place:
//
//	// internal/data/seeder/seed_users.go
//	func seedUsers(ctx context.Context, deps *Deps) error { ... }
//
//	// internal/data/seeder/register.go
//	func Register(r *Registry) {
//		r.Register("items", seedItems)
//		r.Register("users", seedUsers)
//	}
//
// Seeders run in registration order and stop at the first error. Installing
// seed writes no data by itself: `seed` runs only what is registered here. The
// shipped example in seed_items.go seeds the items table `weld add db` creates,
// through the data.Repository the seed graph injects.
//
// A seeder must be idempotent, because seed keeps no ledger and running it twice
// re-runs every seeder. The example checks for an existing row before inserting;
// an upsert (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING) in your own repository is the
// alternative. For generated fake data, use a third-party library such as
// github.com/brianvoe/gofakeit/v7 — weld deliberately ships no faker dependency.
func Register(r *Registry) {
	// weld:seeders:begin
	// Seeders are registered here, in run order. `weld add seed <name>` appends
	// one registration below; edit or reorder them freely, weld only ever
	// appends. Each function lives in its own seed_<name>.go file beside this
	// one.
	r.Register("items", seedItems)
	// weld:seeders:end
}
